  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Living with History, 1914-1964 shows clear early scholarly and professional use. The book has already attracted 3 citations, alongside 1,637 OAPEN downloads, indicating both academic uptake and sustained reader interest. Its strongest thematic resonance is with sustainable cities and communities, aligning the book’s historical analysis of preservation, reconstruction, and urban change with contemporary concerns about heritage and urban policy.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Nicholas Bullock and Luc Verpoest’s Living with History, 1914-1964 examines wartime destruction and post-war reconstruction across Belgium, France, Germany, Great Britain, and the Netherlands, placing preservation debates within broader histories of architecture and urbanism. The record shows that this framing has begun to circulate beyond the book itself: the work has 3 citations in OpenAlex, a modest but meaningful signal that later scholarship is drawing on its historical synthesis.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape suggests especially strong relevance to architecture, urban studies, and spatial-cultural analysis. Later works in Urban Geography and related venues indicate that the book’s ideas continue to inform discussions of place, temporality, and the built environment. This pattern is consistent with the book’s interdisciplinary scope, which connects historiography and history of architecture with questions that are still active in urban research.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage data provide a complementary picture. With 1,637 downloads recorded through OAPEN, the book has been accessed repeatedly by readers, suggesting interest well beyond a single citation stream. That reach matters here because the book addresses a historically grounded but policy-relevant problem: how societies manage destruction, reconstruction, and conservation in cities. The SDG alignment with Sustainable cities and communities reinforces that practical relevance.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the evidence points to a work with early but credible scholarly traction and broader reader uptake. The citation count is still small, so the record supports cautious interpretation rather than strong claims of established field-wide influence. Even so, the combination of downloads, interdisciplinary citations, and sustainability alignment shows that Living with History is being used as a reference point for debates at the intersection of heritage, architecture, and urban change.</p>",
